<Base title="Case Study: Books From Scratch" description="How an established business operating without a real accounting system moved onto QuickBooks, with clean books, reconciled accounts and a monthly close.">
  <div class="container">
    <section class="hero hero-full hero-center">
      <span class="eyebrow boxed">Case Study · First Accounting System</span>
      <h1>Books From <span class="gold-text">Scratch.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">How an established business with no real accounting system moved onto QuickBooks, and what a proper setup changed for the owner.</p>
    </section>

    <article class="article">
      <aside class="glance">
        <h2>At a Glance</h2>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Industry</dt><dd>Owner-led service business</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Starting point</dt><dd>No accounting system; records kept in bank statements, receipts and a spreadsheet</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Engagement</dt><dd>QuickBooks setup, historical cleanup, then ongoing monthly management</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Outcome</dt><dd>Reconciled books, a chart of accounts built for the business, and a monthly report</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <h2>The Business</h2>
      <p>The client is an owner-led business that had been operating successfully for some time. Revenue was steady, customers were paying and bills were getting paid. From the outside, nothing looked wrong.</p>
      <p>What the business did not have was accounting. There was no bookkeeping software and no general ledger. The financial record was the bank statement, a folder of receipts and a spreadsheet the owner updated when time allowed.</p>

      <h2>The Challenge</h2>
      <p>Without a real system, the owner could answer one question with confidence: how much was in the bank today. Everything else took guesswork. Was the business profitable last quarter? Which customers or services made the most money? What was owed to vendors, and what was owed by customers? None of it could be answered without hours of digging.</p>
      <p>The gaps showed up most at tax time. Each year the records had to be pieced together for the tax preparer, which was slow, expensive and stressful, and left the owner unsure whether anything had been missed. Business and personal spending were not always clearly separated, and there was no record of which expenses were deductible.</p>

      <h2>Why This Happens</h2>
      <p>Many businesses start this way, and it is a sensible choice early on. When there are only a handful of transactions, a bank account and a spreadsheet are enough. The trouble is that the business grows and the records do not. By the time the owner notices, setting up proper accounting feels like a large project with no obvious place to start, so it keeps getting put off.</p>
      <p>The common signs:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The bank balance is the only number.</strong> Decisions about hiring, pricing and spending are made from cash on hand.</li>
        <li><strong>Tax season is a scramble.</strong> The year's records are rebuilt once a year, under deadline, instead of kept up as the year goes.</li>
        <li><strong>Lenders and partners ask for statements that do not exist.</strong> A loan, lease or partnership conversation stalls because there is no profit and loss or balance sheet to share.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Approach</h2>
      <h3>Phase 1: Understand the business before building anything</h3>
      <p>bkept started by learning how the business makes and spends money: its revenue streams, its regular vendors, its bank and card accounts, and what the owner and the tax preparer needed to see. The accounting system was designed around those answers rather than a generic template.</p>
      <h3>Phase 2: Set up QuickBooks properly</h3>
      <p>bkept set up QuickBooks Online with a chart of accounts built for the business, so income and expenses are grouped in the way the owner thinks about them and the way the tax return needs them. Bank and credit card accounts were connected through bank feeds, and rules were written for recurring transactions. Business and personal spending were separated going forward.</p>
      <h3>Phase 3: Bring the history in and reconcile</h3>
      <p>With the structure in place, bkept recorded the business's historical activity from bank statements and records, categorized it and reconciled every account to the bank. The result was an opening set of books the owner could trust, rather than a new system stacked on top of unresolved gaps.</p>
      <h3>Phase 4: Hand off to a monthly routine</h3>
      <p>Once the books were current, the engagement moved to ongoing monthly management. Transactions are categorized as they come in, accounts are reconciled every month, and the owner receives a short monthly report with the profit and loss, balance sheet and a plain-language summary of what changed.</p>

      <h2>The Results</h2>
      <p>For the first time, the owner can see how the business is actually performing: profit by month, where the money goes and what is owed in both directions. Year-end now means handing the tax preparer a reconciled set of books instead of a box of statements. And when a lender or partner asks for financial statements, they can be produced the same day.</p>

      <h2>What Changed for the Owner</h2>
      <p>The biggest change was confidence. Questions that used to be put off, about pricing, hiring or whether a slow month was a real problem, now start from numbers instead of instinct. The books stopped being a chore that only came up at tax time and became a tool the owner uses to run the business.</p>

      <h2>Takeaways for Businesses Without an Accounting System</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The bank balance is not the books.</strong> Cash on hand does not show profit, what is owed or where the money went.</li>
        <li><strong>Set it up around the business.</strong> A chart of accounts built for how the business works is what makes the reports useful.</li>
        <li><strong>Clean up the history before moving forward.</strong> Reconciled opening balances mean every report after them can be trusted.</li>
        <li><strong>Software is only half of it.</strong> QuickBooks records the numbers. A monthly routine of review and reconciliation keeps them right.</li>
        <li><strong>It is never too late to start.</strong> Moving from spreadsheets to a real system is a defined project with a clear end, not an open-ended one.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted small-note">The client is not named to protect their privacy. Details are drawn from the engagement.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
